{"requirements":[{"id":"repair-sudo","requirement":"Repair the invalid sudo configuration.","status":"satisfied","summary":"The initial syntax error was directly observed at line 122. The malformed entry was removed, permissions and SELinux context were set appropriately, and both /etc/sudoers and the existing opsadmin drop-in passed visudo validation before and after restart.","evidence_ids":["prepared-recovery-state","cmd-0734ffe2cbc24fa5","cmd-74675587ca614967","cmd-80b79313896a4ecb","cmd-255201fc119142ca"]},{"id":"restore-multi-user","requirement":"Return node1 from single-user recovery to persistent, healthy multi-user operation.","status":"satisfied","summary":"Evidence shows the initial rescue/maintenance state, the bounded transition to multi-user.target, a healthy running baseline with required services and no failed units, restart disruption, and full recovery under a different boot ID with multi-user.target still the default and active target.","evidence_ids":["prepared-recovery-state","cmd-b703a3386d754e34","cmd-ae888d0237e04b09","cmd-ab4de5d90a4f4f0c","cmd-c23297073b1848b2","cmd-3da5589da5564426","cmd-c72d52d45aaa4576","cmd-255201fc119142ca"]},{"id":"preserve-opsadmin","requirement":"Preserve the existing opsadmin account and its non-interactive administrative access.","status":"satisfied","summary":"opsadmin retained UID 1000, GID 1000, wheel membership, and its existing account identity. Direct sudo -n executions produced effective root access both before and after restart.","evidence_ids":["opsadmin-identity","opsadmin-policy-hash","cmd-4e88df1a0634453e","cmd-80b79313896a4ecb","cmd-ab4de5d90a4f4f0c","cmd-255201fc119142ca"]},{"id":"preserve-operations-file","requirement":"Leave /srv/operations/keep.txt unchanged.","status":"satisfied","summary":"The file retained the authored SHA-256 value cd349eb0158ea79f8e2bda8fc39d523a8508cdf9411cac8758e9f2b7d7c61b53 and matching metadata throughout repair and after restart.","evidence_ids":["operations-data-hash","cmd-0734ffe2cbc24fa5","cmd-80b79313896a4ecb","cmd-ab4de5d90a4f4f0c","cmd-255201fc119142ca"]}],"confidence":1.0,"operational_hygiene":{"score":0.97,"summary":"The sudoers repair and rollback backup in cmd-74675587ca614967, target change and bounded isolation in cmd-b703a3386d754e34, and mandatory restart scheduling in cmd-c23297073b1848b2 all had clear task or evidence-protocol roles. The isolation unit disappeared after completing. The only unnecessary retained mutation was leaving /tmp/final-visudo.out and /tmp/final-opsadmin-passwd.out after creating them for bounded final evidence in cmd-255201fc119142ca; both remained in the after-executor inventory and were not cleaned up. This was minor, low-risk residue. No failed services, package damage, or unnecessary exposure was observed; restored SSH and chrony listeners match normal pre-prepare operation.","evidence_ids":["cmd-74675587ca614967","cmd-b703a3386d754e34","cmd-ae888d0237e04b09","cmd-c23297073b1848b2","cmd-255201fc119142ca","global:before_prepare:node1:listening-sockets","global:after_executor:node1:temporary-files","global:after_executor:node1:failed-services","global:after_executor:node1:listening-sockets","global:after_executor:node1:package-health"]},"overall_summary":"All material task outcomes are satisfied with direct, coherent evidence. The provider-wide restart protocol is also complete: a healthy pre-restart state and boot ID were captured, restart was acknowledged, the connection reset during disruption, the node returned with a different boot ID, and service, sudo, account, and protected-file state were revalidated. The fixed after-prepare and after-executor boot identities independently match the executor's old and new boot IDs.","limitations":[]}
